<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lewis Carroll</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charles Lutwidge Dodgson, Dodgson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lewis Carroll was the pen name of Charles Lutwidge Dodgson, an English author, poet, mathematician, photographer and Anglican deacon, born at Daresbury in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>. He studied at Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he lived for most of his life. His Alice's Adventures in Wonderland (1865) and its sequel Through the Looking-Glass (1871) are among the most important works of Victorian literature, and his poems Jabberwocky and The Hunting of the Snark (1876) belong to literary nonsense. Alice Liddell, a daughter of Henry Liddell, the Dean of Christ Church, is widely identified as the inspiration for Alice, though Carroll always denied it. He died of pneumonia in Guildford in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lewis-carroll.jpg" alt="Lewis Carroll" width="330" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lewis Carroll · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LewisCarrollSelfPhoto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dodgson's family was predominantly northern English, conservative and high-church Anglican, and most of his male ancestors were army officers or Anglican clergy. His great-grandfather Charles Dodgson became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Elphin in Ireland, and his father, another Charles, went to Rugby School and Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, won a double first, took holy orders and became a country parson. As a child Dodgson had a fever that left him deaf in one ear, and he acquired a stammer that he called his &quot;hesitation&quot; and kept all his life. At 17 a severe attack of whooping cough probably caused the weak chest of his later years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From a young age Dodgson wrote poetry and short stories, contributing to the family magazine Mischmasch and, between 1854 and 1856, to The Comic Times and The Train. He was ordained a deacon on 22 December 1861, as his fellowship required, but a year later asked the dean for permission not to proceed to the priesthood; Dean Liddell, for unknown reasons, let him remain at the college. In 1856 he took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a> and became a well-known gentleman-photographer. He moved in the Pre-Raphaelite circle, knowing John Ruskin, Dante Gabriel Rossetti and George MacDonald, whose children's enthusiasm for Alice persuaded him to submit it for publication.</p><p>As Mathematical Lecturer at Christ Church he worked in geometry, linear <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, logic and recreational mathematics, producing nearly a dozen books under his real name. In Symbolic Logic Part II he introduced the Method of Trees, the earliest modern use of a truth tree, and he wrote on elections, including what is called Dodgson's method. He also devised the word-ladder puzzle &quot;Doublets&quot;, published in Vanity Fair between 1879 and 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He taught at Christ Church until 1881 and remained in residence there. His last novel, Sylvie and Bruno (1889 and 1893), sold only 13,000 copies and was not appreciated by contemporary readers. He died of pneumonia following influenza on 14 January 1898 at his sisters' home, The Chestnuts, in Guildford, and was buried in the Mount Cemetery there. Four complete volumes of his 13 diaries are missing, and most scholars assume family members removed them, though this is unproven. A memorial stone was unveiled in Poets' Corner, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, in 1982, and an asteroid, 6984 Lewiscarroll, was named for him in 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lewis Carroll” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poem has had many versions and parodies, among them one in Frederick Marryat's novel Poor Jack (1840), another in Mildred J. Hill's Song Stories for the Kindergarten (1896) and &quot;Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Bat&quot;, recited by the Mad Hatter in <b>Lewis Carroll</b>'s Alice's Adventures in Wonderland.</blockquote><a href="/songs/twinkle-twinkle-little-star">Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star</a></li></ul></section>
